# ==========================================================================
#  O68  What the metrics cannot see
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    Metrics and checks are one part of a gate that also runs the reasoner,
#    the OntoClean queries, the style shapes and the competency questions,
#    because a five-out-of-five ontology can still be wrong and no metric
#    sees rigidity or meaning.
#
#  WHY
#    OQuaRE scores the Trail 5 on almost everything, and the Quality
#    Suite's violations come out at zero once the policy questions are
#    decided. Neither knows whether Author is a role, whether Work is one
#    class or two, whether the definitions are circular, whether bs:founded
#    should be functional, or whether the ontology can answer question 7 in
#    the brief. Metrics count what is written; checks pattern-match it.
#    Meaning is not in the graph unless someone put it there, and the parts
#    of this course that put it there, modules 02 and 04, are the parts no
#    metric reads.
#
#    So a gate has more than one instrument. The reasoner: consistent, no
#    unsatisfiable classes, with the whole dataset loaded (o57). The
#    OntoClean queries over the rigidity annotations (o13). The definition
#    audit (o22). The style shapes (o67) and the checks by identifier
#    (o66). And the competency questions, run as queries against the data,
#    which are the only instrument that tests whether the ontology does its
#    job. The SemOps Manual calls them the closest thing ontology work has
#    to a requirement, and makes them executable as .cq.rq tests; this
#    lesson runs three of the brief's questions against the Trail's data as
#    its measured line, and module 12 runs more.
#
#    The critical literature on OQuaRE says the same from the other side:
#    the thresholds were proposed rather than validated, the metrics
#    overlap, and several measure a style choice as if it were a defect,
#    which o65 showed on gist. None of that makes the metrics useless. It
#    makes them one instrument among six, and the one to trust least on its
#    own.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       instrument                    sees                                  blind to
#       OQuaRE metrics                shape and counts                      meaning, rigidity, style choices it counts as absences
#       OQS checks                    patterns, by identifier               whether the pattern is wanted
#       style shapes                  the house style                       whether a definition is true
#       the reasoner                  contradiction, dead classes           errors that are consistent
#       OntoClean queries             rigidity, identity, dependence        anything not annotated
#       competency questions          whether the questions get answered    questions nobody wrote down
#
#       a gate is all six; the last is the one that tests the job
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - A five-out-of-five ontology can be wrong. Metrics count; they do
#      not read.
#    - Six instruments in the gate, and the competency questions are the
#      one that tests whether the ontology does its job.
#    - Trust the metrics least on their own. Their authors said so first.
#
#  CORNER    thought
#  DATA      bookshop-trail-1.1.ttl
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  OPEN IN THE EDITOR
#            https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F10-quality%2Fo68-what-the-metrics-cannot-see.ttl
#  SOURCES   SemOps Manual 8.1 Competency questions as tests
#            https://github.com/pwin/SemOpsManual/blob/main/docs/08-model-and-validate.md#competency-questions-as-tests
#            Duque-Ramos, Fernandez-Breis, Stevens and Aussenac-Gilles, OQuaRE: a SQuaRE-based approach for evaluating the quality of ontologies, JRPIT 43(2), 2011
#            http://www.cs.man.ac.uk/~stevensr/papers/OQuareProof.pdf
#  MEASURED  parses, 6 triples; 'Question 2: towns on the tra' 4 rows; 'Question 1: shops in Wales, ' 4 rows; 'Question 7: authors influenc' 23 rows
# ==========================================================================

@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .

<https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema>
    a                   owl:Ontology ;
    skos:prefLabel      "The Bookshop Trail schema"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote  "The gate: HermiT over the whole dataset; the OntoClean queries; the definition audit; style-shapes.ttl; the Quality Suite scoped to bs:; and the competency questions as queries. Three of the questions run below."@en .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- Question 2: towns on the trail with no bookshop  (on the data)
#  PREFIX bs: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
#  SELECT ?town WHERE { ?town a bs:Settlement . FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?shop bs:locatedIn ?town } } ORDER BY ?town
#
#  -- Question 1: shops in Wales, by the path  (on the data)
#  PREFIX bs: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
#  PREFIX bt: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/>
#  SELECT ?shop WHERE { ?shop a bs:Bookshop ; bs:locatedIn ?town . ?town bs:within+ bt:place-wales } ORDER BY ?shop
#
#  -- Question 7: authors influenced, at any remove, by Rhona Blackwood  (on the data)
#  PREFIX bs: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
#  PREFIX bt: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/>
#  SELECT ?author WHERE { ?author bs:influencedBy+ bt:author-rhona-blackwood } ORDER BY ?author
